@node Tasks
@chapter Google Tasks
@cindex Google Tasks
@cindex OAuth
@cindex @command{raspdash-auth}

The Tasks screen shows your Google Tasks lists, read-only: one column
per list (stacked when the terminal is narrow), subtasks indented under
their parents, due dates on the right and the first line of each task's
notes under it.  A due date is shown in red when it has passed, as
@samp{today} in the accent color, and in yellow when it is at most
@code{soon_days} days away.

raspdash reads the Google Tasks API with the
@code{https://www.googleapis.com/auth/tasks.readonly} scope, so it can
never change your tasks.  Google requires an OAuth client of your own
for that, made once in the Google Cloud console.  The steps below take
about ten minutes.

@section Creating the OAuth client

@enumerate
@item
Open @url{https://console.cloud.google.com/} with the Google account
whose tasks you want to see.  In the project picker at the top choose
@samp{New project}, name it, for example, @samp{raspdash}, and select
it.

@item
Go to @samp{APIs & Services} @result{} @samp{Library}, search for
@samp{Google Tasks API}, open it and press @samp{Enable}.

@item
Go to @samp{Google Auth Platform} (also reachable as @samp{APIs &
Services} @result{} @samp{OAuth consent screen}) and press @samp{Get
started}.  Under @samp{App Information} enter an @samp{App name}
(@samp{raspdash}) and your address as @samp{User support email}.
Under @samp{Audience} choose @samp{External}.  Enter your address
again as contact information, accept the Google API Services User Data
Policy and press @samp{Create}.

@item
Under @samp{Audience} @result{} @samp{Test users} press @samp{Add
users} and add your own Google address.

@item
@cindex refresh token, 7-day expiry
Still under @samp{Audience}, find @samp{Publishing status} and press
@samp{Publish app}, then confirm, so the status reads @samp{In
production}.

@strong{Do not skip this step.}  While the status is @samp{Testing},
Google issues refresh tokens that expire after 7 days; the Tasks
screen would then stop working a week after setup with
@samp{Google token revoked or expired}.  An app @samp{In production}
does not need Google's verification for personal use: Google merely
shows a warning during authorization (see below).

@item
Under @samp{Clients} press @samp{Create client}, choose the
@samp{Application type} @samp{Desktop app}, give it any name and press
@samp{Create}.

@item
In the dialog that follows press @samp{Download JSON}.  Do it right
away: Google may not show the client secret again later, in which case
you would have to add a new secret to the client.  Save the file as
@file{secrets/google_client_secret.json} next to raspdash's
@file{config.toml}.  It must contain an @code{"installed"} section; a
file with a @code{"web"} section belongs to a client of the wrong type.
@end enumerate

@section Authorizing raspdash

@command{raspdash-auth} runs the authorization once and saves a refresh
token.  It needs a web browser, so run it on a computer that has one,
such as your laptop, in a raspdash build or installation with the same
@file{config.toml} and @file{secrets/google_client_secret.json}:

@example
raspdash-auth --config=config.toml
@end example

It listens on a random port of 127.0.0.1, prints a Google URL and tries
to open it with @command{xdg-open}.  Sign in, and if Google says
@samp{Google hasn't verified this app}, choose @samp{Advanced} and then
@samp{Go to raspdash (unsafe)}: you are the developer of this app.
Allow read access to Google Tasks.  The browser comes back to
@command{raspdash-auth}, which writes @file{secrets/google_token.json}
with mode 0600 and exits.

Then copy the token to the Pi, next to the Pi's @file{config.toml}:

@example
scp secrets/google_token.json pi:raspdash/secrets/
@end example

The token file holds only what is needed to get short-lived access
tokens; raspdash keeps those in memory and never writes them to its
cache.  Keep the file private: anyone who has it, together with the
client file, can read your tasks.  To revoke access, remove raspdash at
@url{https://myaccount.google.com/permissions}.

@command{raspdash-auth} accepts these options:

@table @option
@item -c @var{file}
@itemx --config=@var{file}
Read the paths of the client and token files from the @code{[tasks]}
section of @var{file}.
@item --client-secret=@var{file}
@itemx --token=@var{file}
Use these paths instead; with both, no configuration file is needed.
@item -p @var{port}
@itemx --port=@var{port}
Listen on @var{port} instead of a random port.  This allows running
@command{raspdash-auth} on the Pi itself over SSH with a tunnel, for
example @samp{ssh -L 8085:127.0.0.1:8085 pi} and then
@samp{raspdash-auth --port=8085 --no-browser} on the Pi, opening the
printed URL in the laptop's browser.
@item --no-browser
Only print the URL.
@end table

@section Configuration

@example
[tasks]
lists = ["My Tasks"]
show_completed = false
show_assigned = false
show_notes = true
soon_days = 2
client_secret = "secrets/google_client_secret.json"
token = "secrets/google_token.json"
interval = 3600
timezone = "Europe/Moscow"
@end example

@table @code
@item lists
Titles of the lists to show, in this order.  An empty array shows every
list.  Titles that do not exist are named in a warning line above the
lists.
@item show_completed
Also show completed tasks, including those hidden by Google's
@samp{clear completed}, ticked and dimmed.  Default @code{false}.
@item show_assigned
Also show tasks assigned to you from Google Docs or Chat spaces.
Default @code{false}.
@item show_notes
Show the first line of each open task's notes.  Default @code{true}.
@item soon_days
Highlight due dates up to this many days ahead.  Default 2.
@item client_secret
@itemx token
The OAuth client file and the token file, relative to the configuration
file's directory.
@item interval
Seconds between fetches.  Default 3600, once an hour; press @kbd{r} on
the Tasks screen to fetch at once.  The last result is kept in the
cache directory and shown immediately after a restart.  A cache saved
with different @code{lists}, @code{show_completed} or
@code{show_assigned} settings is not shown; raspdash fetches again at
once instead.
@item timezone
Zone that decides which day is @samp{today} for due dates.  Default
@samp{Europe/Moscow}.
@end table

@section Troubleshooting

@table @samp
@item no OAuth client file @dots{}
@itemx no token @dots{}
The file is missing; follow the steps above.
@item Google token revoked or expired; rerun raspdash-auth
The refresh token no longer works: access was revoked, the app was
still in @samp{Testing} (7-day tokens), or it was unused for six
months.  Make sure the app is @samp{In production}, run
@command{raspdash-auth} again and copy the new token to the Pi.
@item Google Tasks HTTP 403 @dots{}
Usually the Google Tasks API is not enabled in the project.
@end table
